<title>SOUTH KOREA: Strikes cost S.Korea 1.18 trln won lost production.</title>
<headline>Strikes cost S.Korea 1.18 trln won lost production.</headline>
<dateline>SEOUL 1997-01-07</dateline>
<text>
<p>South Korea suffered 1.18 trillion won in lost production due to nationwide strikes as of Monday, a trade ministry official said on Tuesday.</p>
<p>Lost exports from the start of industrial action on December 26 were estimated at $244 million, Lee Myong-yup said.  </p>
<p>South Korea's automobile industry lost 886 billion won in production from the strikes, while lost vehicle exports were valued at $135 million, Lee said.</p>
<p>Separately, Hyundai Motor Co's strike-related production loss stood at 235 billion won, Shin Dong-woo, a manager at Hyundai's public relations department, said.</p>
<p>South Korea's major industries have been crippled by strikes launched in protest at the passage of a new labour law.</p>
<p>The law, passed on December 26, allows employers to lay off workers, replace strikers and implement flexible work hours.</p>
<p>($1 = 844 won)</p>
<p>-- Seoul Newsroom (822) 727 5651</p>
